  "analysis": "apt refused to install nodejs because /var/cache/apt/archives has no free space (the overlay reports 0 avail). This is exactly the disk trap the hint warned about. I still need a JavaScript runtime for yt-dlp to bypass YouTube's bot-detection, but apt is blocked. Before deciding how to proceed, I should: (1) confirm whether /app can actually hold a large file (my small write succeeded), (2) free apt's cache, and (3) see what's consuming the disk so I can plan a minimal-footprint approach. Network and /app writes work; I can download nodejs as a precompiled binary to /app instead of using apt.",
  "plan": "First, clear apt's cache and inspect what's consuming space, then test whether /app can actually absorb a sizable file (e.g., 150MB). This tells me if a video download is even viable, or if I must do everything in tiny footprint. Then I'll download a nodejs binary directly (bypassing apt) to satisfy yt-dlp's JS runtime requirement.",
