      "duration": 30
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "yt-dlp still says it found no JS runtime even with --js-runtimes node — it needs the explicit path to the node binary since node isn't on PATH. The flag format is RUNTIME[:PATH]. I'll give the full path to node. YouTube is also using the android vr client; pairing the web client with the node runtime is most likely to bypass the bot check.",
  "plan": "1) Retry yt-dlp with --js-runtimes node:<path> and player_client=web. 2) If it lists formats, inspect available formats and captions. This determines the minimal download needed before committing resources.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 ./yt-dlp -F 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZCbvyPbhRfA' --js-runtimes node:/app/node-v20.11.0-linux-x64/bin/node 2>&1 | tail -30\n",
      "duration": 30
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Node v20.11.0 is at /app/node-v20.11.0-linux-x64/bin/node and runs fine, but yt-dlp still reports 'No supported JavaScript runtime could be found' and fails with YouTube's bot-detection ('Sign in to confirm you're not a bot'). Two issues to resolve: (1) yt-dlp isn't picking up node (the path may need quoting or a different client requires it), and (2) the default android vr client is being bot-blocked. I'll force a browser-like client (web_safari) and explicitly pass the node runtime, with verbose output to see if node is detected.",
  "plan": "Run yt-dlp with --js-runtimes pointing at node (quoted), force player_client=web_safari, and use -v to see whether node is detected and whether the bot check is bypassed. The tail of verbose output will show the runtime detection and the client used.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 ./yt-dlp -F 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZCbvyPbhRfA' --js-runtimes \"node:/app/node-v20.11.0-linux-x64/bin/node\" --extractor-args \"youtube:player_client=web_safari\" -v 2>&1 | tail -25\n",
      "duration": 30
    }
  ]
}

